From 85f9be50f19d43b752cb307f4efac43e3aa39038 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Matteo Pacini <m+github@matteopacini.me>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 23:04:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] Unreal Engine: Render resolution setting, and SR at lower
 render resolutions

Add a "Render Resolution" setting with upscaler presets (Native AA, then
76.9%, 66.7%, 58.8%, 50%, 43.5% and 33.3%) and a custom value. Presets are
named after the selected SR's: XeSS's (Ultra Quality Plus to Ultra
Performance), or FSR's, which has no 76.9% and 43.5% presets, so those are
only listed for XeSS. Presets are saved by index, in the order they were
added.

The setting finds the storage of UE's "r.ScreenPercentage" and
"r.TemporalAA.Upsampling" console variables in the game's executable, from
the code UE generates for TAutoConsoleVariable and cached console variable
lookups, and writes them every frame, so games that reset them from their
own settings don't undo it. "Game Setting" restores the game's latest
values.

While a preset overrides the render resolution, SR isn't set up for dynamic
resolution: FSR then sizes its resources for the render resolution, and
XeSS uses the preset matching it rather than Ultra Performance.

Below about 71%, UE renders views at sizes whose aspect ratio differs more
from the output's (e.g. 1708x960 at 66.7% of 2560x1440), and its TAA jitter
can exceed half a pixel, so the per-view constant buffer was rejected and SR
never ran. Compare aspect ratios with a relative tolerance and accept jitters
up to 2 pixels.

Test builds log, once per reason, why a TAA pass wasn't replaced by SR, and
which TAA shader candidates were rejected for having loops.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
